; case: retry-permitted
; verdict: reject
; ambiguous: yes
; tests: externality=pass implementation-independence=pass non-prescription=fail
; failing-test: non-prescription
; rationale: AMBIGUOUS, resolved REJECT. This looks like a permission rather than a
;   prescription, and permissions feel harmless. It still fails test 3: it names an
;   implementation strategy, so its interpretation cannot be stated without reference to one.
;   It is also redundant — the engine is already free to choose any strategy that meets the
;   contract. A permission field's real effect is to *narrow* the engine to the strategies
;   someone thought of, and to invite a matching prohibition field next.
; other-side: belongs nowhere: the `atomic-observation` contract already admits every strategy
;   that satisfies it
;
(eadl-version eadl/1)

(defservice time.monotonic
  (requires (observation-coherent true))
  (permits (implementation-strategy retry-loop)))
